faq:
  - q: "Is this page telling me what to do?"
    a: "No. Every section shows what the historical record says about a decision from positions like today&rsquo;s &mdash; how often each path worked out, by how much, and the times it didn&rsquo;t. Which path fits a particular situation, horizon and tolerance is the reader&rsquo;s to decide; the tools linked in each section exist so it can be tested with real numbers. The page also takes no inputs at all, which is deliberate: it never knows anything about the reader, so nothing on it can be addressed to one."
  - q: "How is this different from technical analysis?"
    a: "Technical analysis reads price against its own recent pattern. This page measures price against an independent yardstick &mdash; the long-run power law trend &mdash; the way equity investors measure price against earnings. The yardstick moves slowly, is fitted on the whole record, and is falsifiable: the Power Law page states what would break it. Whether that yardstick deserves trust is examined there, not assumed here."
  - q: "Why do some sections show only two past cases?"
    a: "Because only two exist. Price has come to the floor three times in the modern record, and the third time is happening now, so it has no outcome yet. Two closed cases is not enough to compute a distribution from, and this page does not pretend otherwise: rather than publishing a median or a hit rate over two data points, it narrates each visit in full and says plainly that two visits is not a base rate. Where a pooled figure across many samples is shown, it is labelled as pooled and kept separate from the case studies."
  - q: "Why does the page say it has nothing for me if I reject the power law?"
    a: "Because it is accurate rather than dismissive. Every figure here is conditional on the channel being a real long-run structure. A reader who has examined that claim and rejected it is not being told they are wrong &mdash; they are being told this particular map is drawn on a projection they do not accept, which makes it the wrong map for them. That is the page working as designed. The examination itself lives on the Power Law page."
  - q: "Why is there nothing here about borrowing against bitcoin?"
    a: "Because timing, leverage and individual circumstance together make the most dangerous combination on the site, and this page deliberately does not condition borrowing on channel position. Bitcoin as Collateral covers what borrowing against a stack involves, on its own terms and without a position attached. That separation is a design decision, not an oversight."
  - q: "Do the numbers here match the tools they link to?"
    a: "The pooled figures do, exactly &mdash; they come from the same shared engine Wait-or-Deploy and How Much Cash publish from. The case studies do not, and are not meant to: they follow one named entry date forward through the record, which is a different question from the pooled one those tools answer. Each is labelled where it appears, and the method behind the case studies is stated in full in the sources line beneath them."
