  <!-- ══════════ STEP 1 · IS THIS REAL? ══════════ -->
  <section class="rd-section" id="the-premise">
    <div class="rd-step">Step one</div>
    <h2>Is this <em>real</em>?</h2>

    <div class="rd-gate">
      <p>Everything on this page is conditional, and the condition comes first. What follows reads bitcoin&rsquo;s current position in its long-run power law channel and shows what the historical record has said from positions like it. If the power law does not hold, none of it means anything.</p>
      <p>The model, its fit, and what would break it are examined on <a href="/the-power-law">the Power Law page</a>. For a reader who has not made that examination, it comes before this page rather than after.</p>
      <p>For a reader who has made it and rejected the model, this page has nothing to offer &mdash; and that is the page working as designed. A map drawn on a projection you reject is not your map. For a reader who accepts it, holding it loosely is still the better posture: the record below includes the times the pattern bent, and <a href="#what-would-break-this">the section at the bottom</a> names what would break it.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ══════════ STEP 2 · WHAT WOULD IT DO TO MY SITUATION? ══════════ -->
  <section class="rd-section" id="the-record">
    <div class="rd-step">Step two</div>
    <h2>What would it do to my <em>situation</em>?</h2>

    {#- §16.3a THE OPENING BLOCK — a framing block, not a row. The six-part
        anatomy does not apply. It sits after the premise gate and before the
        first row so the historical framing inherits the gate's conditionality
        rather than standing as an independent claim. -#}
    {#- Live-computed lede. It must NOT be static: whether price is at the floor
        right now is a fact that changes, and the sampled record runs up to a few
        weeks behind live spot. A hardcoded "price is at the floor" sentence goes
        false without anyone noticing. The fallback below carries no number and
        no claim about where price is today. -#}
    <p class="rd-lede" id="rdVisitLede">The channel&rsquo;s floor is the position this page opens from &mdash; the record of what has happened there comes before any decision taken from it.</p>

    <p>A visit here is counted the way the rest of the site counts one: an episode in which price came within 1% of the 0.42&times; floor or below it, with a gap of more than about 100 days starting a new episode. That gap rule is not invented for this page &mdash; it is the same rule <a href="/discount-or-premium">Discount, or Premium?</a> uses to separate episodes, adopted here so the two pages cannot report different visit counts for the same history. There is a fourth episode, in the autumn of 2010, and it is left out on purpose: no mature exchange, negligible liquidity, a price in cents. <a href="/the-bitcoin-floor">The Bitcoin Floor</a> records it once and gives it no weight, and so does this page.</p>

    <div class="rd-visits" id="rdVisits"></div>

    <p class="rd-note">Durations are lower bounds. The price series behind every figure here is a roughly 12-day grid rather than a daily close, so an episode measured at 48 days was at least 48 days and may have been longer.</p>

    <!-- ── R1 ── -->
    <div class="rd-row" id="row-deploy">
      <p class="rd-row-q">There is capital to deploy. Has deploying at a position like this one worked out better than waiting for a lower entry?</p>

      <div class="rd-row-part">
        <span class="rd-part-k">What the record shows &mdash; the two closed visits</span>
        <p>Both closed visits are narrated above, each followed forward from its own entry date to one, two and four years. Neither is a distribution and neither is presented as one: two cases cannot support a median, a hit rate, or a spread, and this page does not compute one from them. Where the fourth year has not arrived yet, the table says so rather than estimating it.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="rd-row-part">
        <span class="rd-part-k">The pooled view, kept separate</span>
        <p id="rdR1Pooled"></p>
        <p class="rd-note">That pooled figure answers a different question from the case studies above: it takes every sample the engine matches to this position as one set, rather than following two named visits forward. The two are kept apart deliberately &mdash; splicing them would produce numbers that are individually defensible and collectively incoherent.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="rd-row-part">
        <span class="rd-part-k">The counter-case</span>
        <p id="rdR1Counter"></p>
      </div>

      <div class="rd-row-part">
        <span class="rd-part-k">Sensitivity</span>
        <p>The pooled reading survives moving the matching band 10% in either direction, and holds at every position tested inside this zone. The visit count survives moving the 1% floor-graze threshold 10% either way. Both checks are recorded in the Phase&nbsp;0 report rather than being asserted here.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="rd-row-part">
        <span class="rd-part-k">Where to take it</span>
        <p><a class="rd-route" href="/wait-or-deploy-now">Wait, or Deploy Now? &rarr;</a></p>
        <p class="rd-route-note">That page is the pooled record with the position slider under your own hand &mdash; the exploration, not a check on the case studies above. Its figures will not match them, because it is answering the pooled question. For how to deploy once that is settled &mdash; lump, ladder or hybrid &mdash; <a href="/your-deployment-plan">Your Bitcoin Deployment Plan</a> owns that ground.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="rd-sources">
        <strong>Sources.</strong> Pooled figures: the shared channel-entry engine, the same one <a href="/wait-or-deploy-now">Wait, or Deploy Now?</a> and <a href="/how-much-cash">How Much Cash?</a> publish from &mdash; matched set of <span id="rdR1N">&mdash;</span> samples, a two-year forward window, measured in coins acquired. That engine excludes everything before 2014 as bitcoin&rsquo;s pre-exchange era, so the pooled figures describe the record from 2014 onward, not the whole of it. <strong>Case studies:</strong> no shipped tool computes these, so the method is stated here instead &mdash; each visit&rsquo;s first qualifying sample is taken as the entry, and the price at one, two and four years after that date is read directly from the shared price series the rest of the site uses, interpolating between samples. Plain arithmetic, no fitted parameters, no modelling. Trend and floor from the shared Power Law module.
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ── R3 ── -->
    <div class="rd-row" id="row-cash">
      <p class="rd-row-q">There is already a position. How much cash has it made sense to hold against it from here?</p>

      <div class="rd-row-part">
        <span class="rd-part-k">What the record shows</span>
