    {#- §16.3b THE CODA. After the last row: what is not here yet, named as
        absent. A map, growing — never a matrix, padded. -#}
    <div class="rd-roadmap" id="not-here-yet">
      <h3>What is not here yet</h3>
      <p>This page covers two decisions, and there are more that a holder carries. They are absent for one reason: nothing on this site conditions them on channel position yet, and a section that guessed would be worse than no section. Each arrives when the engine behind it exists and its numbers can be checked against the tool that owns them.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Rebalancing.</strong> <a href="/disciplined-rebalancing">Disciplined Rebalancing</a> works from bands a reader sets themselves, not from where price sits in the channel. Answering &ldquo;are the bands near triggering from here&rdquo; needs the default bands read against live position &mdash; which does not exist.</li>
        <li><strong>Retirement timing.</strong> The <a href="/the-bitcoin-retirement">retirement calculator</a> and the <a href="/the-bitcoin-retirement-stress-test">stress test</a> model withdrawals forward from today. Asking what starting from <em>this</em> position has meant for sequence risk needs the same withdrawal loop run from each historical entry in the zone. That is a real piece of work and the most valuable of the three.</li>
        <li><strong>Selling.</strong> <a href="/discount-or-premium">Discount, or Premium?</a> measures the gap to trend but does not condition outcomes on the position an exit was taken from. It is also the section that would need the most care in the writing: the legitimate reasons people sell &mdash; need, risk reduction, sticking to a plan &mdash; carry full weight, and none of them is a mistake.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="rd-note">A map, growing &mdash; not a matrix, padded. A section is added when an engine exists to compute it from and its figures reproduce on the page that owns them.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ══════════ STEP 3 · WHAT DO WE DO WHEN IT FALLS? ══════════ -->
  <section class="rd-section" id="when-it-falls">
    <div class="rd-step">Step three</div>
    <h2>What happens when it <em>falls</em>?</h2>

    <div class="rd-row" id="row-drawdown">
      <p class="rd-row-q">What has followed entries at this position &mdash; how often did they fall, and how far?</p>

      <div class="rd-row-part">
        <span class="rd-part-k">What the record shows</span>
        <p id="rdR7Body"></p>
      </div>

      <div class="rd-row-part">
        <span class="rd-part-k">The counter-case</span>
        <p>The mildness of those figures is a property of where price already is, not a promise about where it goes. An entry near the floor has less room beneath it than an entry near the trend line, so shallow subsequent falls are close to arithmetic. The record also has nothing to say about a fall <em>through</em> the floor, because it contains none.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="rd-row-part">
        <span class="rd-part-k">Where to take it</span>
        <p><a class="rd-route" href="/the-bitcoin-retirement-stress-test">The Retirement Stress Test &rarr;</a></p>
        <p class="rd-route-note">A what-if rather than a forecast: a crash of a chosen depth, at a chosen year, against a withdrawal plan. It does not compute the figures above and is not where they are checked &mdash; it is where a fall of a size you choose can be put through a plan.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="rd-sources">
        <strong>Sources.</strong> The shared channel-entry engine &mdash; drawdown frequency and median depth over a two-year forward window, matched on today&rsquo;s position, 2014 onward. The same figures Wait, or Deploy Now? renders for the same position.
      </div>
    </div>

    <h3>Holding through it</h3>
    <p>The record above is a record of what followed, not of what it felt like. Every one of those episodes was, at the time, a period in which the reasons to sell were articulate and the reasons to wait were not. That is worth knowing in advance of needing to know it, and it is the limit of what a page of historical figures can offer: the figures describe outcomes, and the decision is made by a person with a mortgage, a job, a family and a tolerance the figures cannot see.</p>
  </section>

  <!-- ══════════ WHAT THIS POSITION DOES NOT CHANGE ══════════ -->
  <section class="rd-section" id="does-not-change">
    <h2>What this position does <em>not</em> change</h2>
    <p>Some decisions are position-insensitive by design, and a page organised around position owes the reader that boundary explicitly. Where price sits in the channel is not an input to any of these.</p>
    <p><strong>How much to hold at all.</strong> Sizing follows tolerance and horizon, not the channel. A position that is too large at 0.4&times; is too large at 2&times;; the channel does not adjust it. <a href="/bitcoin-allocation-sizing">Portfolio allocation</a> is where that is worked out.</p>
    <p><strong>Custody and security.</strong> Nothing in this page&rsquo;s subject matter touches how coins are held, and no channel position makes a custody arrangement better or worse.</p>
    <p><strong>Whether to hold bitcoin at all.</strong> That question is answered by understanding the asset, and it precedes everything here. <a href="/start-here">Start here</a> and <a href="/the-power-law">the Power Law page</a> are where that examination lives. A reader who has not made it is not helped by knowing where price sits relative to a trend they have not yet decided is real.</p>
  </section>

  <!-- ══════════ WHAT WOULD BREAK THIS ══════════ -->
  <section class="rd-section" id="what-would-break-this">
    <h2>What would <em>break</em> this</h2>
    <p>This is the most model-dependent page on the site, and it is worth saying so plainly rather than in a footnote. Every figure above rests on a single model. There is no second method here quietly agreeing with the first &mdash; if the power law is not a real long-run structure, the zone this page is organised around is not a zone, and the visits it counts are not visits.</p>
    <p><strong>The trend is smooth and the path is not.</strong> The channel is a fitted line through fifteen years of violent price history. It describes a central tendency, and no individual entry, exit or holding period is obliged to resemble it.</p>
    <p><strong>The floor is the model&rsquo;s own tripwire.</strong> <a href="/the-bitcoin-floor">The Bitcoin Floor</a> states the criterion in advance &mdash; how far below, for how long &mdash; and reports against it. That criterion is the one this page would fail with, and it is stated there rather than restated here so the criterion has one home rather than two wordings.</p>
    <p><strong>The unsampled scenario is the important one.</strong> The record behind this page contains no instance of the floor giving way and staying gone, so nothing above is evidence that it cannot. The shared engine goes further and clamps sub-floor positions to the floor before matching entries at all, which means a sustained break is not merely rare in this data &mdash; it is structurally absent from it. An absence in a sample is not a property of the world.</p>
    <p><strong>Breaks run in both directions.</strong> A model that can fail downward can fail upward, and the same fifteen years that make the channel plausible are a short record by the standards of the claim being made on them.</p>
  </section>

